---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import WarningPriorityLab from "../../components/WarningPriorityLab.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Warning Priority Lab"
  description="Allocate seven warning types across scarce visual and audio resources, across three driving scenarios."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p>
    A dashboard has a small number of ways to demand attention: one primary
    visual zone large enough to dominate the driver's view, a strict cap on
    how many sounds can play at once before they blur into noise, and one
    slot for a full-screen interrupt that takes over the whole cluster. It
    has seven warnings that might all want one of those at the same time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below, allocate a visual zone, colour tier, sound, persistence, and
    (where you think it's warranted) the full-screen interrupt to each
    warning that's active in the current scenario. The scarce resources are
    enforced live — claiming the primary zone or the interrupt for one
    warning takes it away from whichever warning held it before, and only
    two warnings may sound at once. Switch scenarios to see which warnings
    are even active, and how urgently.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you're satisfied with an allocation, compare it against a reference
    design for that scenario. The comparison isn't a score: it's a rationale
    for a different set of choices, so you can decide where you agree and
    where you don't.
  </p>

  <WarningPriorityLab />
</ContentLayout>
